<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fauj-i-Ain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fauj-i-Ain was the regular standing army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>. Trained along European lines, partly by European officers, it combined disciplined infantry, artillery and cavalry and was the main engine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> expansion. It later became a powerful political force after Ranjit Singh's death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fauj-i-ain.jpg" alt="Fauj-i-Ain" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Singh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sikh_Regular_Infantry_standard.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s reign the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> were made up purely of cavalry. Having become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a> of the Sukerchakia Misl, he unified the misls by diplomacy, marriage alliances and conquest, and his kingdom came to stretch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> in the north-east. In 1805 he began to recruit deserters from the Company as officers and soldiers and to train part of his army in drill, and trained cannoneers from the service of Pierre Cuillier-Perron and Begum Samru joined him.</p><p>A clash at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> in 1808 strengthened his resolve. The Muslim regiment escorting the British envoy Charles Metcalfe held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> procession near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, and in the fracas that followed the Nihangs on guard there were worsted, which impressed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> the need for Western tactics. He hired European officers to train and command parts of his army from 1805 until 1839.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Fauj-i-Ain, a branch of the Sikh Khalsa Army, had two divisions: the Kampu-i-mu'alla (State Troops) and the Fauj-i-Khas (Special Force), an elite with its own emblem and harsh training. Because Sikhs at first looked down on infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> began by recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pathans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a>, and Sikhs joined later. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-ventura">Jean-Baptiste Ventura</a> trained the infantry on the Western pattern from 1822. By 1838–39 it numbered 45,000, in battalions of 800 under a commandant, each of eight companies under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subedar">Subedar</a>, with sections of 25 under a Jamadar.</p><p>General Jean-Francois Allard organised the cavalry, which in 1838–39 numbered 10,000 in regiments of 250 to 600, divided into risalas of 150 to 250, and was paid more than the infantry. The artillery, organised as the Topkhana-i-Khas with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-auguste-court">Claude Auguste Court</a> and Colonel Alexander Gardner, had guns drawn by elephants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, horses or oxen, in batteries of 10 guns and 250 gunners. In 1838–39 it had 182 heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, 20 howitzers and 60 light cannons, with at least 5,000 gunners. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s example, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> later formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of three or four infantry battalions, each with some cavalry and artillery attached.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fauj-i-Ain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Fauj-i-Ain</b>, the regular army, was Ranjit Singh's chief institutional innovation and the instrument of Sikh expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
